## Why This Is Asked

This question probes ability to design a scalable, auditable governance layer that operates within strict per-tenant boundaries while maintaining low latency in KCNA's streaming pipeline.

## Key Concepts

- Policy representation: graph-based rules, versioned, tenant-scoped
- Real-time evaluation: per-record decisions, caching, cache invalidation
- Privacy vs. usefulness: field-level masking vs. full redaction
- Auditability: tamper-evident logs, provenance tagging, verifiable proofs

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: evaluatePolicy(tenantId, topic, record) -> { maskFields: [..], sinks: [..], tags: [..] }
function evaluatePolicy(tenantId, topic, record) {
  const policy = policyCache.get(tenantId, topic);
  // apply masking rules
  for (const f of policy.maskFields) {
    if (record.hasOwnProperty(f)) record[f] = mask(record[f]);
  }
  // attach provenance tags
  record._tags = policy.tags;
  return { masked: record, sinks: policy.sinks };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle policy drift during upgrades without downtime?
- How do you prove policy adherence during audits across backups and restores?

